### What is the K Closest Points problem?

Given n points on a 2D plane and an integer K, return the K points closest to the origin (0,0). Distance is Euclidean: sqrt(x^2 + y^2). The output order does not matter.

At the National Bank of India, the mobile app finds the K nearest ATMs or branches to the customer's GPS coordinates.

```java
package com.bank.geo;

import java.util.*;

public class KClosestPointsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] points = {{3, 3}, {5, -1}, {-2, 4}, {1, 1}, {-1, 0}};
        int k = 3;
        int[][] result = kClosest(points, k);
        System.out.println("K closest: " + Arrays.deepToString(result));
    }

    static int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {
        // Max-heap by squared distance (farthest on top)
        PriorityQueue<int[]> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> Long.compare(dist(b), dist(a))
        );

        for (int[] p : points) {
            maxHeap.offer(p);
            if (maxHeap.size() > k) {
                maxHeap.poll();  // remove farthest
            }
        }

        int[][] result = new int[k][2];
        int i = 0;
        while (!maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
            result[i++] = maxHeap.poll();
        }
        return result;
    }

    static long dist(int[] p) {
        return (long) p[0] * p[0] + (long) p[1] * p[1];
    }
}
```

```text
K closest: [[1, 1], [-1, 0], [-2, 4]]
```

### Why use squared distance instead of true Euclidean distance?

Squaring is monotonic for non-negative values: if sqrt(a) < sqrt(b), then a < b. So comparing squared distances gives the same ordering as comparing true distances, without the cost and floating-point imprecision of square roots. Use `long` for the squared sum to avoid overflow on large coordinates.

```java
// WRONG: floating-point square root (imprecise, slow)
double d1 = Math.sqrt(x1*x1 + y1*y1);
double d2 = Math.sqrt(x2*x2 + y2*y2);

// CORRECT: squared distance (exact, fast)
long d1 = (long) x1 * x1 + (long) y1 * y1;
long d2 = (long) x2 * x2 + (long) y2 * y2;
// d1 < d2 has the same result as sqrt(d1) < sqrt(d2)
```

### Why a max-heap of size K instead of a min-heap of all n?

A min-heap of all n points requires O(n) space and O(n log n) to extract K elements. A max-heap capped at K uses only O(K) space and O(n log K) total time. When n is large and K is small, the difference is significant.

| Approach | Time | Space |
|----------|------|-------|
| Sort all n points | O(n log n) | O(n) |
| Min-heap of all n | O(n + K log n) | O(n) |
| Max-heap of size K | O(n log K) | O(K) |
| QuickSelect | O(n) average | O(n) |

```java
// Max-heap advantage: for n=1,000,000 and K=10
// Max-heap: 1,000,000 * log(10) = ~3.3M operations
// Full sort: 1,000,000 * log(1,000,000) = ~20M operations
```

### How does the max-heap eviction work step by step?

The max-heap keeps the farthest point on top. When a new point is offered and the heap exceeds K, the farthest (top) is polled. After processing all points, the heap contains exactly the K closest.

```java
package com.bank.geo;

import java.util.*;

public class HeapStepByStep {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] points = {{3, 3}, {5, -1}, {-2, 4}};
        int k = 2;

        PriorityQueue<int[]> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> Long.compare(dist(b), dist(a)));

        for (int[] p : points) {
            maxHeap.offer(p);
            System.out.println("Added " + Arrays.toString(p)
                + " (dist=" + dist(p) + "), heap size=" + maxHeap.size());
            if (maxHeap.size() > k) {
                int[] evicted = maxHeap.poll();
                System.out.println("  Evicted " + Arrays.toString(evicted)
                    + " (dist=" + dist(evicted) + ")");
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Result: ");
        while (!maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  " + Arrays.toString(maxHeap.poll()));
        }
    }

    static long dist(int[] p) {
        return (long) p[0] * p[0] + (long) p[1] * p[1];
    }
}
```

```text
Added [3, 3] (dist=18), heap size=1
Added [5, -1] (dist=26), heap size=2
Added [-2, 4] (dist=20), heap size=3
  Evicted [5, -1] (dist=26)
Result:
  [-2, 4]
  [3, 3]
```

### What is the QuickSelect alternative?

QuickSelect partitions the array around a pivot's squared distance. Elements closer than the pivot end up on one side, and farther on the other. By recursing into the correct partition, you find the K-th closest in average O(n) time. However, the worst case is O(n^2).

```java
package com.bank.geo;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class QuickSelectClosest {

    static int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {
        quickSelect(points, 0, points.length - 1, k);
        return Arrays.copyOf(points, k);
    }

    static void quickSelect(int[][] pts, int lo, int hi, int k) {
        if (lo >= hi) return;
        int pivot = partition(pts, lo, hi);
        if (pivot == k) return;
        if (pivot < k) quickSelect(pts, pivot + 1, hi, k);
        else quickSelect(pts, lo, pivot - 1, k);
    }

    static int partition(int[][] pts, int lo, int hi) {
        long pivotDist = dist(pts[hi]);
        int i = lo;
        for (int j = lo; j < hi; j++) {
            if (dist(pts[j]) <= pivotDist) {
                int[] temp = pts[i]; pts[i] = pts[j]; pts[j] = temp;
                i++;
            }
        }
        int[] temp = pts[i]; pts[i] = pts[hi]; pts[hi] = temp;
        return i;
    }

    static long dist(int[] p) {
        return (long) p[0] * p[0] + (long) p[1] * p[1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] points = {{3, 3}, {5, -1}, {-2, 4}, {1, 1}, {-1, 0}};
        int[][] result = kClosest(points, 3);
        System.out.println("K closest: " + Arrays.deepToString(result));
    }
}
```

```text
K closest: [[-1, 0], [1, 1], [-2, 4]]
```

### What is the complete banking implementation?

Here is the ATM finder using the max-heap approach with proper banking context.

```java
package com.bank.geo;

import java.util.*;

public class NearestATMFinder {

    record ATM(String id, int x, int y) {}

    public List<ATM> findNearest(List<ATM> atms, int customerX, int customerY, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<ATM> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> {
            long da = squaredDist(a.x - customerX, a.y - customerY);
            long db = squaredDist(b.x - customerX, b.y - customerY);
            return Long.compare(db, da);  // farthest on top
        });

        for (ATM atm : atms) {
            maxHeap.offer(atm);
            if (maxHeap.size() > k) {
                maxHeap.poll();
            }
        }

        List<ATM> result = new ArrayList<>(maxHeap);
        return result;
    }

    private long squaredDist(int dx, int dy) {
        return (long) dx * dx + (long) dy * dy;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NearestATMFinder finder = new NearestATMFinder();
        List<ATM> atms = List.of(
            new ATM("ATM-001", 1, 2),
            new ATM("ATM-002", 5, 8),
            new ATM("ATM-003", -1, 0),
            new ATM("ATM-004", 3, 3),
            new ATM("ATM-005", 10, 10)
        );
        List<ATM> nearest = finder.findNearest(atms, 0, 0, 3);
        nearest.forEach(a -> System.out.println(a.id() + " at (" + a.x() + "," + a.y() + ")"));
    }
}
```

```text
ATM-003 at (-1,0)
ATM-001 at (1,2)
ATM-004 at (3,3)
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using a min-heap instead of a max-heap, which requires inserting all n points and extracting K, using O(n) space instead of O(K).

```java
// WRONG: min-heap of all n points (O(n) space)
PriorityQueue<int[]> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(
    (a, b) -> Long.compare(dist(a), dist(b)));  // closest on top
for (int[] p : points) minHeap.offer(p);  // all n in heap
int[][] result = new int[k][2];
for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) result[i] = minHeap.poll();  // extract K

// CORRECT: max-heap of size K (O(K) space)
PriorityQueue<int[]> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(
    (a, b) -> Long.compare(dist(b), dist(a)));  // farthest on top
for (int[] p : points) {
    maxHeap.offer(p);
    if (maxHeap.size() > k) maxHeap.poll();  // evict farthest
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Generalization.** This is a K-th smallest problem. Any "top K" or "K-th element" problem can use the same max-heap-of-size-K pattern.
- **Custom origin.** To find K closest to a customer's location instead of the origin, subtract the customer's coordinates before computing squared distance.
- **Tie-breaking.** If two points have the same distance, any tie-breaking is acceptable unless the problem specifies otherwise.
- **Streaming data.** The max-heap approach works for streaming data: you do not need all points in memory at once, just the current K best.
